Every command and every CONFIG.SYS statement PM-DOS has is listed.  A
command's topic says what it does, how it is written, and what each
switch means; Notes and Examples are a key away at the top of it.

                                      
.context About PM-DOS
.context About PM-DOS
.topic About PM-DOS
                                 About PM-DOS
 
PM-DOS is a 32-bit protected-mode operating system built on the
foundation of MS-DOS.  It runs your existing 16-bit DOS programs
without modification, and adds support for 32-bit programs that
can use all of the memory installed in your computer, not just
the first 640K.

PM-DOS also includes features not available in earlier versions
of DOS.  To learn more about a feature, search its name from the
search function or from the main help screen.

Key features:

ş Long File Names.
ş FAT16, FAT32, and exFAT file systems.
ş Built-in memory managers, disk cache, and drivers for mouse and CD-ROM.
ş IDE disks of any size, with UltraDMA up to UltraDMA6 (UltraATA/133).
ş FDISK can create, delete, move, and resize partitions on MBR and GPT
  type disks.
ş Mount disk image and ISO files as drives.
ş USB keyboards, mice and flash drives.
ş Integrated ISA Plug and Play configuration manager.
ş Delete protection support.
ş Native SCANDISK, DEFRAG, DOSSHELL, EDIT, ACROREAD and HELP.
ş ACPI-aware SHUTDOWN.EXE, which flushes the cache and turns off the machine.
ş The PM-DOS shell, CMD.EXE, with DOSKEY features built in.

The command reference is the <\aContents\vContents\v>.  <\aCONFIG.SYS commands\vCONFIG\v>
lists the statements CONFIG.SYS takes, and <\aStarting PM-DOS\vStarting\v>
says what happens when the machine starts.
 
                                     
.context Help on Help
.context h.pg1
.context How to Use PM-DOS Help
.topic Help on Help
.topic How To Use PM-DOS Help

                            How To Use PM-DOS Help

 Choose a topic below.

 <\aMoving Through PM-DOS Help\vNavigating\v>
 <\aFinding Text in PM-DOS Help\vSearching\v>
 <\aHelp on Menus and Dialogs\vContext Help\v>
 <\aUsing the Menus\vMenus\v>
 <\aUsing a Dialog Box\vDialogs\v>
 <\aStarting PM-DOS Help from the Command Line\vHelp Command Line\v>
 <\aUsing PM-DOS Help with the Keyboard\vHelp Keys\v>

 To read a topic, click it.  Or press TAB until the cursor is on it,
 and then press ENTER.

 To leave PM-DOS Help, choose Exit from the File menu: press ALT, then
 F, then X.

.context Navigating
.context Moving Through PM-DOS Help
.topic Navigating
.topic Moving Through PM-DOS Help

                          Moving Through PM-DOS Help

 To scroll the text, click the arrows on the scroll bar, or press PAGE
 DOWN and PAGE UP.

 Links

 A link leads to another topic.  Links are shown between brackets, like
 this: <\aDIR\vDIR\v>.  Many topics start with links to their Notes and their
 Examples, which stay at the top of the window while the rest scrolls.

 To move the cursor to the next link

 ş Press TAB.  Or press the first letter of the link.

 To move the cursor to the previous link

 ş Press SHIFT+TAB.  Or press SHIFT and the first letter of the link.

 To read the topic a link leads to

 ş Click the link.  Or move the cursor onto it and press ENTER.

 To see the contents

 ş Click Contents on the bottom line, or press ALT+C.

 To read the topic after this one

 ş Click Next on the bottom line, or press ALT+N.  The topics are in
   the order the help file has them, which is the contents' order.

 To go back to the topics you have read

 ş Click Back on the bottom line, or press ALT+B.  Each press goes
   back one more topic.

.context Searching
.context Finding Text in PM-DOS Help
.topic Searching
.topic Finding Text in PM-DOS Help

                          Finding Text in PM-DOS Help

 Find, on the Search menu, looks for a piece of text in every topic of
 the help file, not only the one on the screen.

 Type the text and choose OK.  The search starts after the cursor and
 goes on through the topics that follow, round to the start of the
 file and back to where it began.  When the text is found, the topic it
 is in is shown with the cursor on it.  Press F3 to find the next one.

 Match Upper/Lowercase finds only text written with the same capitals.
 Whole Word skips the text when it is part of a longer word.

 With nothing found, PM-DOS Help says "Match not found".

.context Context Help
.context Help on Menus and Dialogs
.context gah
.topic Context Help
.topic Help on Menus and Dialogs

                           Help on Menus and Dialogs

 F1 says what the thing in front of you is for.

 To get help on a menu

 ş Press ALT to go to the menu bar, move to the menu with the LEFT and
   RIGHT ARROW keys, and press F1.

 To get help on a command in a menu

 ş Open the menu, move to the command with the UP and DOWN ARROW keys,
   and press F1.

 To get help on a dialog box

 ş Press F1, or choose its Help button.

 The help comes up in a box over whatever you were doing; choose OK to
 get back to it.

.context Menus
.context Using the Menus
.topic Menus
.topic Using the Menus

                               Using the Menus

 The menu bar at the top of the screen has three menus: File, Search
 and Help.

 To open a menu           With the mouse: click its name.
                          With the keyboard: press ALT, then the letter
                          shown in white in its name.  Or press ALT and
                          move to it with the LEFT and RIGHT ARROW keys,
                          then press ENTER or DOWN ARROW.

 To choose a command      With the mouse: click it.
                          With the keyboard: press its white letter, or
                          move to it with the arrow keys and press ENTER.

 To close a menu          Press ESC, or click somewhere else.

 <\aFile Menu\vFile Menu\v>  <\aSearch Menu\vSearch Menu\v>  <\aHelp Menu\vHelp Menu\v>

.context Dialogs
.context Using a Dialog Box
.topic Dialogs
.topic Using a Dialog Box

                              Using a Dialog Box

 Find and Print ask what you want in a dialog box.

 To move to a part of the box    Press TAB, or SHIFT+TAB to go back.
                                 Or press ALT and the white letter in
                                 its name.  Or click it.

 To choose an option             Move to the group with TAB and use the
                                 arrow keys.  Or click the option.

 To turn a check box on or off   Move to it and press the SPACEBAR, or
                                 click it.

 To press a button               Move to it and press ENTER, or click
                                 it.  ENTER anywhere in the box presses
                                 the button with the white brackets.

 To close the box doing nothing  Press ESC, or choose Cancel.

.context Help Command Line
.context Starting PM-DOS Help from the Command Line
.topic Help Command Line
.topic Starting PM-DOS Help from the Command Line

                   Starting PM-DOS Help from the Command Line

 The HELP command starts PM-DOS Help.

 ş Type HELP on its own for the contents.

 ş Type HELP and the name of a command or a CONFIG.SYS statement to
   open at its topic.  For example:

   help copy
   help country

 A name PM-DOS Help does not know gets "Match not found", and then the
 contents.

 HELP reads its text from HELP.HLP, which is looked for in the current
 directory, then where HELP.EXE itself is, then along the PATH.  The
 file is plain text and can be changed with EDIT; the <\aHELP\vHELP\v> topic
 says how it is laid out.

